{:edb/db-before-t 1895 :edb/db-after-t 1896 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535208 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:33:33.896Z" 13194139535208 true] [17592186092323 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337671 13194139535208 true] [17592186092323 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337678 13194139535208 true] [17592186092324 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337685 13194139535208 true] [17592186092324 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337692 13194139535208 true] [17592186092419 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535208 true] [17592186092419 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059427/assets/5117030d23370b2167513b7a2eee5258aae4eb9f45ad428fd82e4334c4ce6638.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1+3t,\\,y=2{t}^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" 13194139535208 true] [17592186092419 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/q-10260.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 1 + 3t, y = 2t^{3/2}, t \\in  [0, 1]$." 13194139535208 false] [17592186092419 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{8}-2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535208 true] [17592186337671 1017 #edb/ref 17592186337672 13194139535208 true] [17592186337671 1089 "q-123027" 13194139535208 true] [17592186337671 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535208 true] [17592186337671 1100 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$ ?" 13194139535208 true] [17592186337671 1113 #uuid "a37e6009-e864-56bc-93ae-b166a2b05514" 13194139535208 true] [17592186337671 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=8-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(8-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+64-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535208 true] [17592186337672 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337673 13194139535208 true] [17592186337672 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337674 13194139535208 true] [17592186337672 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337675 13194139535208 true] [17592186337672 1000 #edb/ref 